# Affiliate redirects — rule 12.
#
# Every affiliate link on this site points at /go/<slug>, never at the
# destination. This file is what turns that into a real redirect, served by
# Cloudflare's static asset handler at the edge: no Worker script, no
# JavaScript, no request logging, nothing stateful.
#
# MUST AGREE WITH src/lib/affiliates.ts, line for line. Same slugs, same URLs.
# scripts/check-deploy-config.mjs fails the build if they diverge — two files
# that have to say the same thing with no check between them is what shipped
# every page on this site as a 307 (D46).
#
# FORMAT:  /go/<slug>   <destination>   302
#
# 302, NOT 301. A permanent redirect is cached by browsers indefinitely, so the
# day a programme changes its destination — or ends — every visitor who ever
# clicked the old one keeps going there, and nothing we deploy can reach them.
# Affiliate destinations are the least permanent URLs on the internet.
#
# Crawlers are kept off these paths by `Disallow: /go/` in robots.txt, and the
# links themselves carry rel="sponsored nofollow" (rule 12), enforced against
# the built HTML by scripts/check-links.mjs.
#
# THERE ARE NO PARTNERS YET. That is deliberate: the convention exists before
# the first link does, so nothing has to be retrofitted across a grown site.
# Adding one means an entry here AND in src/lib/affiliates.ts.

# ── Migration redirects — sources that must no longer be built ───────────────
#
# Format:  /old-path   /new-path   301
#
# scripts/check-deploy-config.mjs asserts, for every line below: the source is
# ABSENT from dist/, the destination is PRESENT in it, and the status is 301.
# The first of those is the one that matters — Cloudflare serves a real asset in
# preference to a redirect rule, so a rule whose source is still built is
# silently dead and every other gate stays green.
#
# THIS BLOCK IS A PROBE, NOT A MIGRATION. Nothing has moved yet. The single line
# below exists to prove the mechanism serves a live 301 at all before nine real
# URLs are staked on it. `public/_headers` shipped broken from the first commit
# for exactly this reason (D45): Cloudflare parses it at deploy time, after CI,
# so nothing in this repository had ever read it. No rule in this file has ever
# served in production either.
#
# Remove this line once the probe has returned 301 and the result is recorded.
/_redirect-probe   /about   301
